{:answer/id #uuid "225422b7-65e9-5615-8767-2543d225f63d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "greatest upper bound" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488539} {:answer/id #uuid "a21eac77-4a8d-5a1a-a8b3-704e5197e4df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "greatest lower bound" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488790} {:answer/id #uuid "b2347c38-baed-5481-b764-dca5a77c4889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "smallest lower bound" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488826} {:answer/id #uuid "d62ffa3e-3570-5c8f-89e2-cacd170877a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "smallest upper bound" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a21eac77-4a8d-5a1a-a8b3-704e5197e4df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "greatest lower bound" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488790} :answer-field/id #uuid "efa87f6a-f0a6-55ea-84b0-cbaaaa5fa7b9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "993667c9-c973-5dd1-b35f-eb1848f951dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-253557" :question/problem "Consider the set $A=(-5,5].$ Complete the following reasoning to identify $inf(A).$\n\nThe infimum of $A$ is the {{field-1}} of $A.$\n\nThe set of lower bounds of $A$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\le -5$ is smaller than or equal to the elements of $A.$ Thus, the set $(-\\infty ,-5]$ contains only lower bounds of $A.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x>-5$ is **not** a lower bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is smaller than $x$ for any $x>-5.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-5]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,-5]=-5.\n$$\n\nNotice that $-5\\notin A.$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of $A$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $A.$\n\nThe set of lower bounds of $A$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x\\le -5\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $inf(A)=-5\\notin A.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489264 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162158 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffe2b2c0-23a0-5bd0-8bc7-c12139820b61" :knowledge-point/title "Supremum and Infimum of a Sequence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07ca890c-4ce7-564d-a6a4-a9bac71abb91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sup(A)=3\\notin A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488487} {:answer/id #uuid "5e011c7d-1483-5d78-9bc4-5f179586ff35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sup(A)=3\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488651} {:answer/id #uuid "dc4faa51-f0ba-59dd-a17c-8673a8ed7588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sup(A)=\\frac{21}{5}\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488926} {:answer/id #uuid "f17c6a34-e689-50a4-8b50-91c915977c45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sup(A)=\\frac{21}{5}\\notin A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488981} {:answer/id #uuid "fef7ef80-e0c5-5157-bd1a-b6b0059c3156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$sup(A)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc4faa51-f0ba-59dd-a17c-8673a8ed7588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sup(A)=\\frac{21}{5}\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488926} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7ca4c4c-da25-5075-bde9-61051e330507" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7f8c921-7225-588c-b9e8-a78affb7e519" :question/math-academy-id "q-253843" :question/problem "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{3+2{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=3+2{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/05/05737c6e8e7b88f1a91a5fd6cc7d5b6a088227e327ef66e937e279c090a47256.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing and converges to $3.$ The maximum of $A$ is \n\n$$\nmax(A)={a}_{1}=3+2{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{1}=\\frac{21}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $x\\ge {a}_{1}$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x<{a}_{1}$ cannot be an upper bound because ${a}_{1},$ an element of $A,$ is greater than $x.$\n\nTherefore, $[\\frac{21}{5},\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\nsup(A)=min[\\frac{21}{5},\\infty )=\\frac{21}{5}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\frac{21}{5}$ is the maximum of $A.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nsup(A)=\\frac{21}{5}\\in A.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489295 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162159 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4649783b-3436-55a5-b779-1ca3de17c535" :knowledge-point/title "Supremum and Infimum of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b8257c3-cb89-57e7-af23-191b90a42228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b8257c3-cb89-57e7-af23-191b90a42228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488647} :answer-field/id #uuid "60ad18e2-13e5-50fe-b40b-8175f7273995" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489065}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd9f0c45-62d6-5bab-b897-93c9f4793a8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-253869" :question/problem "![](images/5d/5dc63d364beae0482fdb996372e5042e4c5edf2a6bdbd7a5b79dbcc55cf419cb.png)\n\nWhat is the supremum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n${sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a function is the supremum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=(-1,4].$\n\nTo find the supremum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\ge 4$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y<4$ cannot be an upper bound of $A$ because $4,$ an element of $A,$ is greater than $y.$\n\nTherefore, $[4,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=sup(A)=min[4,\\infty )=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489208 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162159 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4649783b-3436-55a5-b779-1ca3de17c535" :knowledge-point/title "Supremum and Infimum of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d76112fe-0fce-56ee-bf7a-c6fba9800dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d76112fe-0fce-56ee-bf7a-c6fba9800dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488914} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ed2c775-df54-54e2-ad43-b85dd389a975" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42308119-ffcd-55c6-b052-68e017e7cc90" :question/math-academy-id "q-253897" :question/problem "![](images/64/64131e649a82e06d2750d53bdf05b1701b0674a05b70149081ddb58d700adb54.png)\n\nWhat is the supremum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n${sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a function is the supremum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=(-1,2).$\n\nTo find the supremum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\ge 2$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y<2$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is greater than $y$ for any $y<2.$\n\nTherefore, $[2,\\infty )$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=sup(A)=min[2,\\infty )=2.\n$$"}]}
